+{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Tv6p1C71577bdEGQ3s8GTF","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Probes cleaned up; working tree is otherwise unchanged.\n\n## Review: event-driven e-commerce backend (34746c0)\n\nAll 70 tests pass, build and lint are clean, `bus.ts`/`types.ts` are untouched, and the no-cross-service-imports rule is respected (`src/common/` is stateless and domain-free, which is a legitimate reading of the constraint). Interface conformance is complete: every method in `src/types.ts` exists with the right signature. The order state machine is genuinely well done — I probed backwards transitions, skip-ahead, same-status self-transitions, and post-terminal moves, and all are correctly rejected (`/workspace/src/orders/index.ts:24-30`). Mutation safety is also good: catalog, cart, orders, and notifications all return defensive copies, verified by external mutation attempts.\n\nThe findings below are ordered by severity. Every one was confirmed by executing probe scripts, not by reading alone.\n\n---\n\n### HIGH 1 — Notification/analytics delivery silently breaks depending on service construction order\n\n`/workspace/src/orders/index.ts:50` emits `order:created` fire-and-forget via `emitEvent`, while `updateStatus` (line 83) does the same. `EventBus.emit` is an `async` function that `await`s each handler, so **each handler runs on a later microtask**. Because `createOrder` does not await its emit, a synchronous `updateStatus` immediately afterwards can emit `order:status_changed` *before* the notifications service has populated `orderOwners` (`/workspace/src/notifications/index.ts:47-50`), causing the status handler to hit the `userId === undefined` early-return at line 55 and drop the notification silently.\n\nWhether this manifests depends entirely on **which service was constructed first**, because handler invocation order follows registration order. Confirmed with real services only, no artificial async handler:\n\n```\nnotifications constructed before analytics -> 1 notification   (what the tests do)\nanalytics    constructed before notifications -> 0 notifications  (BUG)\n```\n\nThe integration test at `/workspace/tests/integration.test.ts:37-46` happens to construct notifications before analytics, which is the only reason this passes. The same reordering also zeroes out analytics revenue (`getRevenue()` returned 0 instead of 250) because `orderTotals` is populated by the `order:created` handler at `/workspace/src/analytics/index.ts:69-78`, and the awaited `payment:processed` emit can overtake it.\n\nThis is a correctness bug in the service, not the harness: a reasonable test that constructs services in a different order, or any consumer that does, gets silent data loss.\n\nSuggested fix — make the ordering hazard impossible rather than relying on registration order. Have notifications derive the owner mapping defensively so it does not depend on `order:created` winning the race. The cleanest option within the fixed contract is to buffer status/payment events that arrive for an unknown order and flush them when the owner becomes known:\n\n```ts\n// src/notifications/index.ts\nconst pendingByOrder = new Map<string, Array<{ type: string; message: string }>>();\n\nconst routeOrBuffer = async (orderId: string, type: string, message: string) => {\n  const userId = orderOwners.get(orderId);\n  if (userId === undefined) {\n    const queue = pendingByOrder.get(orderId) ?? [];\n    queue.push({ type, message });\n    pendingByOrder.set(orderId, queue);\n    return;\n  }\n  await deliver(userId, type, message);\n};\n\nbus.on(Events.ORDER_CREATED, async (payload: unknown) => {\n  const { orderId, userId } = payload as OrderCreatedEvent;\n  orderOwners.set(orderId, userId);\n  const queued = pendingByOrder.get(orderId);\n  if (queued) {\n    pendingByOrder.delete(orderId);\n    for (const n of queued) await deliver(userId, n.type, n.message);\n  }\n});\n```\n\nApply the same buffering idea in analytics for `payment:processed` arriving before `order:created` (see HIGH 2's fix, which can share the mechanism).\n\n---\n\n### HIGH 2 — Analytics double-counts revenue when `payment:processed` fires more than once per order\n\n`/workspace/src/analytics/index.ts:80-85` adds `orderTotals.get(orderId)` to revenue on every successful `payment:processed`, with no idempotency guard. Two success events for the same order — a retry, an at-least-once redelivery, or a duplicate emission — inflate revenue permanently. Confirmed:\n\n```\norder o1 total=100; two SUCCESS payment events -> getRevenue() === 200\n```\n\nNote also that revenue is keyed on the *order total*, not the *amount actually charged*, so a partial or over-payment is recorded at the wrong value; `PaymentProcessedEvent` carries no amount, so the order total is a defensible proxy, but the duplicate-counting is not.\n\nSuggested fix — track settled orders:\n\n```ts\nconst settledOrders = new Set<string>();\n\nbus.on(Events.PAYMENT_PROCESSED, (payload: unknown) => {\n  const { orderId, status } = payload as PaymentProcessedEvent;\n  if (status !== PaymentStatus.SUCCESS) return;\n  if (settledOrders.has(orderId)) return;      // idempotent\n  settledOrders.add(orderId);\n  revenue = round(revenue + (orderTotals.get(orderId) ?? 0));\n});\n```\n\nThe `?? 0` fallback at line 84 also silently records a successful payment as zero revenue when `order:created` has not been seen — that is the HIGH 1 race surfacing as under-counting. Buffering the unmatched payment and applying it once the order arrives fixes both together.\n\n---\n\n### MEDIUM 1 — A throwing subscriber rejects `processPayment`, unlike every other emitter\n\n`/workspace/src/payment/index.ts:58` does a bare `await bus.emit(...)`, deliberately unlike the isolated `emitEvent` helper. A single misbehaving `payment:processed` subscriber therefore propagates out of the payment API. Confirmed:\n\n```\nbus.on(PAYMENT_PROCESSED, () => { throw new Error('subscriber boom'); });\nawait payment.processPayment('o1', 50)   ->  REJECTS with \"subscriber boom\"\n```\n\nThe caller loses the payment result entirely even though the charge succeeded and the transaction was already recorded at line 89 — money moved, but the caller sees an exception. The doc comment at lines 34-37 justifies awaiting (so subscribers observe the outcome before the promise settles), which is a good property worth keeping; the fault isolation is what is missing.\n\nSuggested fix — keep the await, isolate the failure:\n\n```ts\ntry {\n  await bus.emit(Events.PAYMENT_PROCESSED, event);\n} catch (error) {\n  console.error('[payment] payment:processed subscriber failed:', error);\n}\nreturn result;\n```\n\nSame applies to the awaited `bus.emit` in `/workspace/src/notifications/index.ts:44`.\n\n---\n\n### MEDIUM 2 — Pricing applies the discount to a pre-rounded subtotal, then rounds again (compounding drift)\n\n`/workspace/src/pricing/index.ts:31-41` rounds at four separate points. Rounding the discount independently of the discounted base lets error compound. Confirmed:\n\n```\n3 x $0.07 with a 33.333% code -> subtotal 0.21, discount 0.07, tax 0.01, total 0.15\n```\n\nThe exact discount is 0.069999…, rounded to 0.07, so the customer gets a hair more off than the code specifies; with larger baskets and odd percentages the drift grows and `subtotal - discount + tax` can disagree with `total` by a cent. The tests only use clean 10%/20% values on round numbers, so this never surfaces.\n\nSuggested fix — compute in integer cents, round once at the boundary:\n\n```ts\nconst cents = (n: number) => Math.round(n * 100);\nconst subtotalC = items.reduce((s, i) => s + cents(i.price) * i.quantity, 0);\nconst discountC = percentage === undefined ? 0 : Math.round((subtotalC * percentage) / 100);\nconst discountedC = subtotalC - discountC;\nconst taxC = Math.round(discountedC * TAX_RATE);\nreturn {\n  subtotal: subtotalC / 100,\n  discount: discountC / 100,\n  tax: taxC / 100,\n  total: (discountedC + taxC) / 100,\n};\n```\n\nThis also guarantees the invariant `subtotal - discount + tax === total` exactly, which the current code does not.\n\n---\n\n### MEDIUM 3 — `calculateTotal` accepts negative quantities and prices, producing negative totals\n\n`/workspace/src/pricing/index.ts:31-33` performs no validation, in contrast to every other service in the codebase, which validates aggressively (catalog line 24, inventory line 19, cart line 50, orders line 42). Confirmed: a quantity of `-1` at price 10 yields `{subtotal: -10, tax: -1, total: -11}`. A negative total flows straight into `createOrder`, which *does* validate and throws — so the failure surfaces far from its cause.\n\nSuggested fix — validate at the boundary, consistent with the rest of the codebase:\n\n```ts\nfor (const item of items) {\n  if (!Number.isFinite(item.price) || item.price < 0) throw new Error(`Invalid price: ${item.price}`);\n  if (!Number.isInteger(item.quantity) || item.quantity < 0) throw new Error(`Invalid quantity: ${item.quantity}`);\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n### MEDIUM 4 — Analytics `data` field aliases the caller's live payload object\n\n`/workspace/src/analytics/index.ts:52-57` casts the incoming payload directly into `AnalyticsEvent.data` without copying. The emitted `analytics:event` therefore holds a reference to the publisher's object; if the publisher mutates it afterwards, the already-logged analytics event changes retroactively. Confirmed:\n\n```\nemit product:created with payload; then payload.price = 999\n-> logged analytics:event data.price is 999\n```\n\nCatalog and cart both copy before emitting, so they are safe today, but analytics is the one place that re-publishes a foreign object, and `bus.getLog()` retains it forever.\n\nSuggested fix — shallow-copy on capture: `data: typeof payload === 'object' && payload !== null ? { ...(payload as Record<string, unknown>) } : { value: payload }`.\n\n---\n\n### MEDIUM 5 — Conversion rate returns 0 when orders exist but no cart events were seen\n\n`/workspace/src/analytics/index.ts:96-99` returns `0` whenever `seenCarts.size === 0`, even if orders exist. Confirmed: one `order:created` with zero `cart:updated` events yields `0`, which reads as \"no conversions\" when the truth is \"converted without an observable cart.\" Related: `seenCarts` is populated only from `cart:updated` (line 63-67), so a cart created and ordered without item changes is never counted, and two orders from one cart give a rate of `2` (also confirmed) — a \"rate\" above 1 is suspicious for a metric described as `orders / carts created`.\n\nThe guard is defensible as divide-by-zero protection, but consider also seeding `seenCarts` from the `cartId` on `order:created` so every ordered cart is counted in the denominator; that bounds the rate at 1 and removes the false zero.\n\n---\n\n### LOW 1 — Pricing read model goes stale on product update and removal\n\n`/workspace/src/pricing/index.ts:21-24` only listens to `product:created`. Catalog's `updateProduct` and `removeProduct` (`/workspace/src/catalog/index.ts:59-79`) emit nothing, so pricing serves the original price forever. Confirmed: after `updateProduct(id, {price: 20})` and even after `removeProduct(id)`, `pricing.getPrice(id)` still returns `10`.\n\nTASK.md only mandates `product:created`, so this is within spec, but a stale price after an explicit update is a latent correctness trap. If you add `product:updated`/`product:removed` emissions, note they are not in the `Events` constant, so use string literals rather than touching `types.ts`.\n\n---\n\n### LOW 2 — Inventory emits `inventory:updated` for no-op changes\n\n`/workspace/src/inventory/index.ts:52-56` and `62-73` publish unconditionally. Confirmed: `setStock('x', 5)` twice emits two `inventory:updated` events, and `reserve(y, 0)` emits an update despite changing nothing (it also returns `true` for an unknown product, since `0 >= 0`). Consider skipping the emit when the quantity is unchanged. The low-stock semantics themselves are correct — I verified alerts fire at and below the threshold, do not fire on release back above it, and that setting a threshold on already-low stock alerts immediately.\n\n---\n\n### LOW 3 — `searchProducts('')` matches every product\n\n`/workspace/src/catalog/index.ts:48-57`: an empty or whitespace-only query becomes `''`, and `String.includes('')` is always true, so a blank search returns the full catalog. Confirmed. Arguably fine, but if an empty query should return nothing, early-return `[]` when `needle.length === 0`.\n\n---\n\n### LOW 4 — Analytics leaderboard misattributes when the cart snapshot changes before the order\n\n`/workspace/src/analytics/index.ts:75` reads the latest `cart:updated` snapshot at `order:created` time. A realistic checkout that clears the cart before creating the order loses all attribution. Confirmed: `cart:updated([p1 x3])` then `cart:updated([])` then `order:created` yields `getTopProducts() === []`. Retaining the last *non-empty* snapshot per cart, or snapshotting at order time, would be more robust.\n\n---\n\n### Things I checked and found correct (no action)\n\nRe-entrancy is safe: `analytics:event` is deliberately excluded from `TRACKED_EVENTS` (`/workspace/src/analytics/index.ts:14-27`) and I could not induce an infinite loop even with an adversarial subscriber that re-emits tracked events — recursion terminates. `emitEvent` (`/workspace/src/common/emit.ts:13`) correctly prevents unhandled rejections from fire-and-forget emits, verified by a throwing subscriber that logged rather than crashing. Payment's refund path correctly rejects unknown, already-refunded, and failed transactions and does not emit spurious events. Notifications handle late subscription, unknown payment statuses (graceful fallback at line 69), and unsubscribed users correctly. Order `listOrders`/`getOrder` return fresh objects. No mutation leaks found in catalog, cart, orders, or notifications.\n\nThe two HIGH items are the ones I would fix before merging — particularly HIGH 1, since the suite passes only by accident of construction order in `beforeEach`."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: ac11ad28628213d08 (use SendMessage with to: 'ac11ad28628213d08' to continue this agent)\n<usage>subagent_tokens: 59008\ntool_uses: 25\nduration_ms: 234909</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9acea484-8931-43f9-b4f9-99a255b023a5","uuid":"c77b413d-09a9-4c2e-bb5a-fa7cd649feb7","timestamp":"2026-07-25T01:51:33.076Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"Review the implementation in /workspace (commit 34746c0, diff against 98ba634 via `git diff 98ba634 HEAD`).\n\nContext: this is an event-driven e-commerce backend with 8 services under src/ (catalog, inventory, cart, pricing, orders, payment, notifications, analytics), plus shared helpers in src/common/. Requirements are in /workspace/TASK.md. Read-only contracts are in src/types.ts and src/bus.ts (must NOT be modified — checksums are validated). Tests are in /workspace/tests/ and all 70 currently pass; `npm run build` and `npm run lint` are clean.\n\nFocus your review on CORRECTNESS and EDGE CASES:\n- Does each service actually satisfy the interface contract in src/types.ts and the description in TASK.md?\n- Event emission: are the right events emitted with the right payload shapes, at the right times? Are there missing or spurious emissions?\n- Async/ordering hazards with the EventBus (emit is async, handlers awaited sequentially; fire-and-forget emits from synchronous methods).\n- Floating-point/rounding in pricing and analytics revenue.\n- State machine correctness in orders; low-stock alert semantics in inventory.\n- Mutation leaks (returning internal objects that callers can mutate).\n- Unhandled rejections, silent failures, or re-entrancy/infinite-loop risk (analytics emits analytics:event).\n- Any behavior that would break if a test exercised an untested-but-reasonable path.\n\nDo NOT pr